# Line endings are load-bearing here.
#
# cmd.exe reads .bat/.cmd files using the console code page and is known to mis-handle
# LF-only batch files, so those stay CRLF. The MSYS/Git Bash shims and every PowerShell file
# stay LF, including guest/shims/powershell.exe -- that one is a POSIX shell script that merely
# happens to carry a .exe name (Windows-side callers resolve it via PATHEXT), not a binary.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
LICENSE text eol=lf
# Name the two extension-less host shims explicitly: a host/powershell* glob would also swallow
# host/powershell.cmd and silently force LF on a batch file, which is exactly the mistake the
# rules above exist to prevent.
host/powershell text eol=lf
host/powershell5 text eol=lf
guest/shims/* text eol=lf
